  "Number": 12,
  "Question": "When \\(\\left(1-\\dfrac1a\\right)^6\\) is expanded, the sum of the last three coefficients is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(22\\)",
    "B": "\\(11\\)",
    "C": "\\(10\\)",
    "D": "\\(-10\\)",
    "E": "\\(-11\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "For \\(a\\ne0\\), expand in increasing powers of \\(1/a\\). The last three terms come from choosing \\(-1/a\\) from four, five, or six of the six factors. By the binomial theorem, they are\n\\[\n\\binom64\\left(-\\frac1a\\right)^4\n+\\binom65\\left(-\\frac1a\\right)^5\n+\\binom66\\left(-\\frac1a\\right)^6\n=\\frac{15}{a^4}-\\frac6{a^5}+\\frac1{a^6}.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\binom64=\\binom62=6\\cdot5/2=15\\), while the other two binomial coefficients are \\(6\\) and \\(1\\). Including the signs, the requested sum is \\(15-6+1=\\boxed{10}\\), choice C.",
